To implement a multiple-choice dialogue in a text-based Java game, you can follow these steps:

1. Create a `Dialogue` class to represent a dialogue with multiple choices. This class should have the following fields:

- `String text`: The text of the dialogue.
- `List<String> choices`: A list of choices for the player to select from.
- `int correctChoiceIndex`: The index of the correct choice, if there is one.

2. Create a `DialogueManager` class to manage the dialogues and handle user input. This class should have the following methods:

- `void showDialogue(Dialogue dialogue)`: Display the dialogue to the user and handle their input.
- `void showChoices(List<String> choices)`: Display the choices to the user.
- `int getUserChoiceIndex()`: Get the index of the choice selected by the user.

3. In your game loop, call the `showDialogue` method of the `DialogueManager` to display a dialogue to the user.

Here's an example implementation of the `Dialogue` and `DialogueManager` classes:

```java
import java.util.List;
import java.util.Scanner;

class Dialogue {
    String text;
    List<String> choices;
    int correctChoiceIndex;

    public Dialogue(String text, List<String> choices, int correctChoiceIndex) {
        this.text = text;
        this.choices = choices;
        this.correctChoiceIndex = correctChoiceIndex;
    }
}

class DialogueManager {
    private Scanner scanner;

    public DialogueManager() {
        this.scanner = new Scanner(System.in);
    }

    public void showDialogue(Dialogue dialogue) {
        System.out.println(dialogue.text);
        showChoices(dialogue.choices);
        int userChoiceIndex = getUserChoiceIndex();
        if (dialogue.correctChoiceIndex != -1 && userChoiceIndex == dialogue.correctChoiceIndex) {
            System.out.println("Correct!");
        } else {
            System.out.println("Incorrect. The correct answer was " + dialogue.choices.get(dialogue.correctChoiceIndex));
        }
    }

    public void showChoices(List<String> choices) {
        for (int i = 0; i < choices.size(); i++) {
            System.out.println((i + 1) + ". " + choices.get(i));
        }
